Diagnosis: generate_bcd_labels in data.py computes the distance transform on the full-size volume (125×1250×1250) separately for every single instance — and sample A alone has 37,366 instances. SciPy's distance_transform_edt allocates a full-volume-sized working array each call, so it's not just slow (37,366 full-volume passes), it's also why the very first call already blew past available RAM. The bug isn't the shape fix — it's that this function never crops to each instance's bounding box before processing.

The fix: crop to each instance's bounding box (via scipy.ndimage.find_objects), run erosion/distance-transform on that small crop only, then write the result back into the correct offset of the full-size output array. This fixes both the memory error and what would otherwise be a very slow function (tens of thousands of full-volume operations reduced to tens of thousands of small-crop operations).

