Switch to Agent mode if not already on. The prompt is ready to run.

Fix a memory bug in generate_bcd_labels() in src/data.py. Do not touch
model.py, losses.py, decode.py, metrics.py, train.py, infer.py, or
evaluate.py. Do not change the function's inputs, outputs, or the meaning
of B/C/D channels -- only change HOW they are computed internally.

Current bug: for every distinct instance id in instance_labels, the function
calls binary_erosion(mask) and distance_transform_edt(mask) where mask is
the SAME SIZE AS THE FULL VOLUME (e.g. 125x1250x1250), even though each
instance only occupies a small region of it. With 37,366 instances in one
CREMI volume, this means 37,366 full-volume distance-transform calls, each
allocating large temporary arrays -- this is what caused the
ArrayMemoryError (trying to allocate 4.37 GiB) and would also make this
function extremely slow even if memory were not an issue.

Required fix: use scipy.ndimage.find_objects(instance_labels) to get each
instance's bounding-box slice ONCE (a single call returns all bounding
boxes). For each instance id:
  1. Get its bounding box slice tuple from the find_objects result.
  2. Pad that bounding box by 1 voxel on each side (clipped to the volume's
     actual bounds) so erosion/distance-transform near the crop edge is
     still correct.
  3. Extract the small cropped sub-volume: crop = instance_labels[padded_slice]
  4. Compute mask = (crop == inst_id) on this SMALL crop only.
  5. Run binary_erosion(mask) and distance_transform_edt(mask) on this small
     crop -- not the full volume.
  6. Write the resulting small contour and distance values into the
     corresponding location of the full-size `contour` and `dist_map`
     output arrays, using the same padded_slice offset. For contour, use
     logical OR with whatever is already there (in case of adjacent
     bounding boxes overlapping at the padding border). For dist_map, use
     np.maximum with whatever is already there, not += (avoid double-adding
     in any overlapping padded regions).

